    {
      "id": "claim_7384e62518c8449ba1279c929bb50a0b",
      "statement": "The agent's executeCode env is built from the chat's binding map as a plain object (the loader's serializer rejects anything else, so prototype-pollution safety comes from validateBindingName instead — invalid names are logged and skipped); workpiece entries resolve to gadget or gatekeeper loopbacks by what still exists, and agent-callback entries embed the stored args (already containing TransientStubLoopback fetchers) directly in env.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://packages/workshop-backend/src/overseer.ts#L2748-L2792",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:577427cc5f82d401af711cccb348158435951546b5908c6c113f349157864c8b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_225eb25663c34676bcb62153528bb290",
      "statement": "Persistent callback stubs are created with ctx.restore(params) against a symbol-named [restore] method and can be stored in Durable Object storage; when agent code (not gadget code) needs to forge such a stub against a gadget, the overseer loads a one-off restore-forger worker whose ctx.restore call is made while pretending to be the target gadget, so the forged stub restores through that gadget's [restore], and until a runtime unsealing API exists the placeholder throws if called but becomes real once stored and read back.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://packages/workshop-backend/src/overseer.ts#L108-L155",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:87a418735a726d2b2fd6332e8f3db9afbfbf95ce1507178d6aa07d010c2eeb0b:"
        },
        {
